## 2️⃣ Alternative: `CustomVectorSource` + `CustomVectorTileProvider`

If you **don’t want a URL at all** (or you already have the tile coordinates in code), you can skip the whole HTTP layer and create a source directly from Kotlin:

```kotlin
import org.maplibre.android.maps.Style
import org.maplibre.android.style.sources.CustomVectorSource
import org.maplibre.android.style.sources.CustomVectorTileProvider
import org.maplibre.android.style.sources.TileData
import kotlinx.coroutines.CoroutineScope
import kotlinx.coroutines.Dispatchers
import kotlinx.coroutines.launch

// Inside your Activity/Fragment after the map style is loaded:
mapboxMap.getStyle { style ->

    val vaultSource = CustomVectorSource(
        id = "vault",
        provider = object : CustomVectorTileProvider {
            // This runs on a background coroutine (you can also use suspend functions)
            override suspend fun fetchTile(z: Int, x: Int, y: Int): TileData {
                val bytes = Vault.decryptTile(z, x, y)   // your decryption routine
                return TileData.Mvt(bytes)
            }
        },
        scope = CoroutineScope(Dispatchers.IO),   // where the fetch runs
        maxZoom = 14                               // optional, set to your max zoom
    )

    style.addSource(vaultSource)

    // Add a layer that uses the source (same as you would in JSON)
    val fillLayer = FillLayer("my-data", "vault")
        .withSourceLayer("my_layer")
        .withProperties(
            PropertyFactory.fillColor(Color.parseColor("#ff6600")),
            PropertyFactory.fillOpacity(0.5f)
        )
    style.addLayer(fillLayer)
}
```

*Pros*: No URL parsing, no need to touch the HTTP stack.  
*Cons*: You lose the ability to reference the source from a remote style JSON; you must add the source programmatically.

---

## 3️⃣ Alternative: OkHttp interceptor (if you already use OkHttp)

If your app already configures an `OkHttpClient` for other networking, you can let MapLibre use that client and answer the `vault://` requests there.

```kotlin
import okhttp3.OkHttpClient
import okhttp3.Protocol
import okhttp3.Response
import okhttp3.ResponseBody.Companion.toResponseBody
import org.maplibre.android.module.http.HttpRequestUtil

val client = OkHttpClient.Builder()
    .addInterceptor { chain ->
        val request = chain.request()
        // OkHttp only sees http/https URLs, so we encode the vault request
        // in a query param or a custom header. Example: https://example.com/blank?vault=12/3456/7890
        val vaultParam = request.url.queryParameter("vault")
        if (vaultParam == null) {
            // Not a vault request → normal network
            return@addInterceptor chain.proceed(request)
        }

        // Parse z/x/y from the query param
        val (zStr, xStr, yStr) = vaultParam.split('/')
        val z = zStr.toInt()
        val x = xStr.toInt()
        val y = yStr.toInt()

        // Decrypt the tile
        val bytes = Vault.decryptTile(z, x, y)

        // Build a synthetic HTTP 200 response
        Response.Builder()
            .request(request)
            .protocol(Protocol.HTTP_1_1)
            .code(200)
            .message("OK")
            .body(bytes.toResponseBody(null))   // MIME type can be null for MVT
            .build()
    }
    .build()

// Tell MapLibre to use this client (available in every release)
HttpRequestUtil.setOkHttpClient(client)
```

**How to keep the style JSON unchanged?**  
MapLibre will only pass URLs that it can parse as `http`/`https`. Therefore you need a tiny “shim” URL that the style can reference, e.g.:

```json
{
  "type": "vector",
  "url": "https://example.com/blank?vault={z}/{x}/{y}"
}
```

The interceptor sees the `vault` query parameter, decrypts the tile, and returns the bytes. The dummy host (`example.com`) can be anything; the request never leaves the device because the interceptor short-circuits it.

*Pros*: Re-uses existing OkHttp pipeline, easy to add logging, caching, etc.  
*Cons*: Requires a “placeholder” URL in the style; you cannot use a pure `vault://` scheme.

